{"name":"Crystal Cut","hex":"#f8f4ed","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"crystal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A solid composed of an array of atoms or molecules possessing long-range order and arranged in a pattern which is periodic in three dimensions.","A piece of glimmering, shining mineral resembling ice or glass."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of crystal.","Very clear; coherent."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crystal"},{"term":"cut","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Teutila Cuicatec."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To incise, to cut into the surface of something.\nTo perform an incision on, for example with a knife.\n\nTo divide with a knife, scissors, or another sharp instrument.","To perform an incision on, for example with a knife."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cut"}],"entries":[{"title":"Crystal","blocks":[{"text":"A crystal or crystalline solid is a solid material whose constituents are arranged in a highly ordered microscopic structure, forming a crystal lattice that extends in all directions. In addition, macroscopic single crystals are usually identifiable by their geometrical shape, consisting of flat faces with specific, characteristic orientations. The scientific study of crystals and crystal formation is known as crystallography. The process of crystal formation via mechanisms of crystal growth is called crystallization or solidification."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Quartz_28.jpg/330px-Quartz_28.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystal","wikidata":"Q43533"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Sodium-chloride-3D-ionic.png/500px-Sodium-chloride-3D-ionic.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimed